Introduction {#intro}
============

This paper proposes making `std::exception` and publicly derived exception
types formattable with `std::format` and directly printable with `std::print`.
It also specifies the encoding of library-generated parts of standard exception
messages, resolving [[LWG4087]].

Motivation {#motivation}
==========

Exception messages are commonly written to logs or diagnostic output. Today
this requires explicitly calling `what()`:

```c++
try {
  process_request();
} catch (const std::exception& e) {
  std::println(stderr, "request failed: {}", e.what());
}
```

More importantly, calling `what()` is not a portable workaround. Consider a
nonexistent path on Windows:

```c++
try {
  (void)std::filesystem::file_size(L"Шчучыншчына");
} catch (const std::exception& e) {
  std::println("Памылка: {}", e.what());
}
```

Even when the ordinary literal encoding is UTF-8 and the terminal supports
Unicode, different parts of the message returned by `what()` may use a legacy
code page or mutually incompatible encodings. `std::println` has no way to
determine those encodings, so the output can be mojibake or fail to display.
This breaks an otherwise Unicode-capable path from `std::filesystem::path` to
formatted terminal output.

The proposal also makes all parts of the message whose encoding is controlled
by the standard consistently use the ordinary literal encoding, preventing
mojibake caused by combining library-generated text in incompatible encodings.

`std::exception` itself cannot be passed to a formatting function even though
it has a standard textual representation. The proposed formatter makes the
common case more direct:

```c++
try {
  process_request();
} catch (const std::exception& e) {
  std::println(stderr, "request failed: {}", e);
}
```

Encoding {#encoding}
========

The encoding of standard exception messages at runtime is currently
unspecified. &bs[;[exception](https://eel.is/c++draft/exception)] only says
that `what()` returns an implementation-defined null-terminated byte string and
requires the ordinary literal encoding during constant evaluation. The remarks
that the message may be a multibyte string suitable for conversion to
`std::wstring` do not identify its encoding. The unspecified encoding makes
portable interpretation and display of exception messages impossible in
general.

[[LWG4087]] tracks this problem. It points out that an exception message cannot
be used portably without a known encoding and proposes requiring standard
exception messages to use the ordinary literal encoding.

Implementation practice {#encoding-impl}
-----------------------

A survey of libstdc++, libc++ and Microsoft STL confirms that there is no
single de facto encoding for exception messages:

* Fixed messages such as those returned by `bad_alloc::what()` are generally
    ordinary string literals containing only ASCII characters.

* `logic_error`, `runtime_error` and their subclasses preserve the bytes
    supplied to their constructors without validation or transcoding.

* POSIX system-category messages normally come from `strerror` or `strerror_r`
    in the current C locale. On Windows, some implementation versions obtain
    system-category messages from `FormatMessageA` and may therefore use the
    process Active Code Page (ACP). This behavior is version-dependent and
    inconsistent: libc++ used
    `strerror` through LLVM 19, while Cygwin continues to use the `strerror`
    path. `FormatMessageA` output
    has to be converted when its code page differs from the execution character
    set.

* Filesystem error messages can additionally contain paths whose encoding is
    platform- and implementation-dependent.

In libstdc++, `filesystem_error` appends
[`path::u8string()`](https://github.com/gcc-mirror/gcc/blob/da15e5fec25eeb2c3163ddf7de11a1036da03b96/libstdc%2B%2B-v3/src/c%2B%2B17/fs_path.cc#L2064-L2084)
to the underlying `system_error` message. On POSIX this preserves native path
bytes, while on MinGW it converts wide paths to UTF-8. Converting the system
text as required by the standard still leaves a mismatch unless the execution
character set is UTF-8.

libc++ copies native path bytes on POSIX. On Windows it formats wide paths with
[`%ls` through narrow `vsnprintf`](https://github.com/llvm/llvm-project/blob/10a37889d4d14a30a7fef19e61acf8b8808ddeae/libcxx/src/filesystem/format_string.h#L21-L50),
delegating conversion to the C runtime locale. Its Windows system category also
uses `FormatMessageA`.

Microsoft STL stores paths as UTF-16 and converts them to UTF-8, an OEM code
page or the ACP depending on runtime state
([conversion selection](https://github.com/microsoft/STL/blob/eae5df4865853ce20a5013b29514dc32c6a04de3/stl/src/filesystem.cpp#L244-L253)).
Its system category independently uses
[`FormatMessageA`](https://github.com/microsoft/STL/blob/eae5df4865853ce20a5013b29514dc32c6a04de3/stl/src/syserror_import_lib.cpp#L38-L75),
while the path conversion can still select a different encoding.

[[P3395]] improves the situation by specifying the encoding of
`error_category::message()`. However, it does not ensure that a complete
`filesystem_error::what()` or `system_error::what()` string has a single
encoding. The surveyed implementation sources also illustrate the changes
needed to implement the new requirement.

Proposed encoding model {#encoding-model}
-----------------------

This paper resolves [[LWG4087]] by requiring every library-generated part of a
standard exception message to use the ordinary literal encoding. This encoding
is appropriate because exception messages are commonly combined with ordinary
string literals and passed to formatting and output facilities that interpret
narrow strings accordingly. Unlike the execution character set, the ordinary
literal encoding does not change in response to `setlocale` while an exception
object is alive.

Using the ordinary literal encoding also facilitates future formatting support
for additional code unit types: library-generated text can be transcoded from a
known, stable source encoding instead of relying on encoding detection.

`filesystem::path` already provides [`display_string()`](
https://eel.is/c++draft/fs.path.native.obs), which returns a string suitable for
formatting and printing in the ordinary literal encoding. There is no
corresponding operation that converts a path to the execution character set
(the active C locale's encoding); `native_encoded_string()` instead uses the
operating-system-dependent pathname encoding. The ordinary literal encoding
therefore reuses an existing path conversion rather than requiring a new one.

Performing conversion only in the formatter would leave existing direct uses of
`e.what()` broken. Of the solutions considered here, requiring the stored
representation itself to use the ordinary literal encoding is the only one that
preserves the `const char*` interface and its use as ordinary narrow text while
fixing both direct and formatted output.

[[P3395]] specifies the encodings of error-category text.
&bs[;[syserr.errcat.virtuals](
https://eel.is/c++draft/syserr.errcat.virtuals)] requires
`error_category::name()` to return a string in the ordinary literal encoding
and `error_category::message()` to return a string of multibyte characters in
the execution character set.

This is a source encoding, not a persistent presentation encoding.
`error_category::message()` creates a new string for the current C locale,
whereas `what()` exposes stored text that must remain valid after the locale
changes and cannot be re-encoded by its non-throwing accessor. [[P3395]]
therefore provides the source encoding needed by `system_error` to transcode
the message when constructing its stable `what()` representation. It does not
specify the encoding of the complete result, which can also contain an
arbitrary user-provided `what_arg`, or of paths incorporated into
`filesystem_error::what()`.

The NTBS supplied through a `what_arg` parameter is incorporated without
changing its code-unit values. This preserves the existing `strcmp`
postconditions and does not reinterpret arbitrary user data. If every such
NTBS uses the ordinary literal encoding, the complete result of `what()` does
as well. Otherwise, the encoding of the complete result is not guaranteed,
although all library-generated parts remain in the ordinary literal encoding.

When a `system_error` incorporates an error-category message, it converts the
message to the ordinary literal encoding during construction, capturing the
execution encoding before a later locale change. A `filesystem_error` similarly
converts an included pathname during construction using the existing
`display_string()` conversion.

Construction-time conversion handles later sequenced locale changes without
requiring work in the non-throwing `what()` function. Supporting an independently
changing locale during conversion or incompatible ordinary literal encodings
across communicating translation units would require encoding metadata that the
current `what()` interface cannot provide.

The requirement applies when the dynamic type of the exception object is a
standard library class. Program-defined exception types remain responsible for
the encoding of their own messages.

The formatter passes the result of `what()` to the standard string formatter
without transcoding and is provided only for `char`. This is necessary because
the formatter also applies to program-defined exceptions and to standard
exceptions containing arbitrary program-supplied character data.

Proposal {#proposal}
========

The current paper proposes the encoding model described in
[Encoding](#encoding), together with a `formatter` partial specialization for
`std::exception` and types publicly derived from it. The default format is the
string returned by the virtual `what()` member function. The specialization
uses the standard string format specification, providing the usual fill,
alignment, width, precision, and debug formatting.

For example:

```c++
std::runtime_error e("connection refused");
auto s1 = std::format("{}", e);       // "connection refused"
auto s2 = std::format("{:>20}", e);   // "  connection refused"
auto s3 = std::format("{:?}", e);     // "\"connection refused\""
```

As discussed in [Encoding](#encoding), the formatter is provided only for
`char` because `what()` returns a narrow string and does not provide enough
information to portably transcode it to `wchar_t`.

The formatter is not opted into the nonlocking formatter optimization because
`what()` is virtual and can execute program-defined code.

Wording {#wording}
=======

Modify paragraph 2 of &bs[;[exception](
https://eel.is/c++draft/exception)] as indicated:

The `what()` member function of each such `T` satisfies the constraints
specified for `exception::what()` (see below).

<ins>
For a call to `what()` on an object whose dynamic type is a standard library
class that is `exception` or is derived from `exception`, every character
sequence incorporated into the returned NTBS, other than an NTBS supplied by
the program and copied without modification, is in the ordinary literal
encoding. If every such copied NTBS is in the ordinary literal encoding, the
returned NTBS is in the ordinary literal encoding.
</ins>

Modify `exception::what()` in &bs[;[exception](
https://eel.is/c++draft/exception)] as indicated:

```
constexpr virtual const char* what() const noexcept;
```

*Returns*: An implementation-defined NTBS, which during constant evaluation is
encoded with the ordinary literal encoding.

*Remarks*: <del>The message may be a null-terminated multibyte string, suitable
for conversion and display as a `wstring`.</del> The return value remains valid
until the exception object from which it is obtained is destroyed or a
non-`const` member function of the exception object is called.

Modify `system_error::what()` in &bs[;[syserr.syserr.members](
https://eel.is/c++draft/syserr.syserr.members)] as indicated:

```
const char* what() const noexcept override;
```

*Returns*: An NTBS incorporating the arguments supplied in the constructor.

<ins>
For a constructor with a `what_arg` parameter, the NTBS designated by
`what_arg.c_str()` or `what_arg`, as applicable, is incorporated without
changing its element values. If an error-category message is incorporated, then
during construction let `m` be `code().message()` and let `E` be the encoding
of the execution character set in effect when that call returns. `m` is
transcoded from `E` to the ordinary literal encoding during construction and
incorporated into the returned NTBS. The handling of characters not
representable in the ordinary literal encoding is implementation-defined.
</ins>

Modify `filesystem_error::what()` in
&bs[;[fs.filesystem.error.members](
https://eel.is/c++draft/fs.filesystem.error.members)] as indicated:

```
const char* what() const noexcept override;
```

*Returns*: An NTBS that incorporates the <del>`what_arg` argument supplied to
the constructor</del><ins>NTBS designated by `what_arg.c_str()` without
changing its element values</ins>. The exact format is unspecified.
Implementations should include the `system_error::what()` string and the
pathnames of `path1` and `path2` <del>in the native format in the returned
string</del><ins>in the returned string. For each included pathname
corresponding to a path `p`, `p.display_string()` is evaluated during
construction and its result is incorporated into the returned string</ins>.

The feature-test macro below detects the formatter addition. The encoding
changes are intended to be applied as a defect report and do not depend on the
macro.

Add an entry for `__cpp_lib_format_exception` to section "Header `<version>`
synopsis" &bs[;[version.syn](https://eel.is/c++draft/version.syn)], in a place that
respects the table's current alphabetic order:

<pre>
<ins>#define __cpp_lib_format_exception 20XXXXL // also in &lt;format></ins>
</pre>

Modify "Header `<format>` synopsis" &bs[;[format.syn](
https://eel.is/c++draft/format.syn)] as indicated:

<pre>
  // [format.formatter], formatter
  template&lt;class T, class charT = char> struct formatter;

  <ins>// [format.exception], exception formatter
  template&lt;class T>
    requires derived_from&lt;T, exception>
  struct formatter&lt;T, char>;</ins>

  // [format.formatter.locking], formatter locking
  template&lt;class T>
    constexpr bool enable_nonlocking_formatter_optimization = false;

  <ins>template&lt;class T>
    requires derived_from&lt;T, exception>
  constexpr bool enable_nonlocking_formatter_optimization&lt;T> = false;</ins>
</pre>

Add a new section "Exception formatter" [format.exception] under
&bs[;[format.formatter](https://eel.is/c++draft/format.formatter)]:

<pre>
template&lt;class T>
  requires derived_from&lt;T, exception>
struct formatter&lt;T, char> : formatter&lt;const char*, char> {
  template&lt;class FormatContext>
    constexpr typename FormatContext::iterator
      format(const T& e, FormatContext& ctx) const;
};
</pre>

The specialization is debug-enabled and constexpr-enabled.

<pre>
template&lt;class FormatContext>
  constexpr typename FormatContext::iterator
    format(const T& e, FormatContext& ctx) const;
</pre>

*Returns*: `formatter<const char*, char>::format(
static_cast<const exception&>(e).what(), ctx)`.

Implementation experience {#impl}
=========================

A formatter for `std::exception` itself, as well as types derived from it, is
available in the open-source {fmt} library ([[FMT]]).

